'''Xylomiges''' (zī-lom′i-jēz), ''n.'' [NL. (Guenée, 1852, as ''Xylomyges''), < Gr. ξυλομιγής, mixed with wood, < ξύλον, wood, + μιγνύναι, mix.] A genus of noctuid moths, of the family ''Apamidæ'', comprising species of moderate size, robust body, short proboscis, and palpi hardly reaching above the head. {{smaller|The genus is wide-spread, but contains only about a dozen species, of which 9 inhabit the United States. See ''silver-cloud.''}}

'''xylonite''' (zī′lọ̄-nīt), ''n.'' [Irreg. < Gr. ξύλον, wood, + ''-ite''.] Same as ''celluloid''.

'''Xylonomus''' (zī-lon′ọ̄-mus), ''n.'' [NL. (Gravenhorst, 1829), < Gr. ξύλον, wood, + νέμειν, graze, feed.] An important genus of hymenopterous parasites, of the ichneumonid subfamily ''Pimplinæ'', having very long legs and antennæ, and the marginal cell of the fore wing extending nearly to the apex of the wing. {{smaller|The species are rather large, are wide-spread, and are parasitic upon the larvæ of the larger wood-boring beetles, such as the ''Cerambycidæ''; 15 are known in Europe, and 9 have been described from the United States.}}

